{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"agent_message","text":"Under the Irish framework, applying ESMA’s *Guidelines on ETFs and other UCITS issues*, section IX, paragraphs 22–24:\n\n- **Exceptional direct redemption:** A secondary-market investor must be allowed to redeem directly with the ETF where its exchange price **significantly diverges from NAV**—for example, during market disruption involving an absent market-maker. Mere preference for direct dealing is insufficient.\n- **Notification:** The relevant regulated market must be informed that the ETF is open for direct redemptions.\n- **Process and costs:** The prospectus must disclose the procedure for these redemptions and potential costs. Those costs must not be excessive; redemption is not necessarily free.\n\n**Worth knowing:** Ordinarily, secondary-market holders buy and sell through an intermediary on-exchange, rather than redeeming with the fund. Required investor disclosures explain intermediary fees and the possibility of buying above NAV or selling below NAV."}}
